Feature: Robot Framework
  Robot Framework 7.5 with the Browser library 21.0.0 and its Chromium, from the Browser library's published image by tag and digest, runs the tree's suites in a namespace of its own that a cluster's overlay selects, at the restricted Pod Security level with a read-only root and no service account token: an HTTP suite and a UI suite against the demo's hello and, by a second piece a cluster without Ascender leaves out, a UI suite of Ascender's sign-in page, each target a substitution variable passed to Robot as a variable, as the CronJob robot-suites at 05:45 UTC every night and as just robot-run on demand. Each run writes its reports into a dated directory of a claim, the newest thirty runs are kept and an index lists them newest first, and a failed test fails the job, which the stock KubeJobFailed reports. A headless Chromium opens whatever page a suite names, unsandboxed under the restricted level, and the reports hold screenshots and whatever the suites print, so what holds it is where the runner may send, who reads the reports, and that no suite holds a credential or signs in: the namespace denies traffic by default, the runner reaches the cluster's resolver and its targets and nothing else once the cluster enforces the policies, each target admits it by a rule beside it, the report server, busybox's httpd serving the claim read-only, takes only the host, nothing routes to it or scrapes it, and just robot forwards a local port on 127.0.0.1 to it until Ctrl-C.

  Scenario: Robot Framework is selected by overlay entries, builds nothing when it is not, and builds without Ascender
    Given the lab-sto2 overlay, which selects Robot Framework with three entries, the namespace directory robot and the ks.yaml of the applications robot and robot-ascender
    When it is built with all three, with only the namespace directory's, with none, without Ascender's entries and robot-ascender's, without Ascender's alone, without robot's alone and without cilium
    Then with all three it applies the namespace robot, its app-template-defaults ConfigMap, its default-deny NetworkPolicy and the Kustomizations robot, which waits, names cilium alone and substitutes ROBOT_TARGET_HELLO and ROBOT_TARGET_ASCENDER, and robot-ascender, which deploys into robot, waits, names cilium, robot and ascender and substitutes nothing; with only the namespace directory's, the namespace, the ConfigMap and the NetworkPolicy; with none, nothing of the namespace; without Ascender and robot-ascender, everything of robot, whose one object that names Ascender is its CronJob, by the unused variable and the optional suite volume; and without Ascender alone, without robot alone or without cilium it is refused, naming robot-ascender and ascender, robot-ascender and robot, or robot and cilium

  Scenario: Robot Framework's runner and report server run images pinned by tag and digest
    Given the robot application built as Flux applies it, Renovate's record of what it extracts and the repository's Renovate rules
    When the images of its pods and the rules that name them are read
    Then the CronJob robot-suites and the Deployment robot-reports make every pod, one container each; the runner runs docker.io/marketsquare/robotframework-browser and the server docker.io/library/busybox, each by a release tag of three numbers and a sha256 digest; Renovate's kubernetes manager reads both from their manifests, with no comment of its own and no entry of tag forms; one rule keeps Renovate to the Browser library's tags of three numbers, which 21, 21.0 and latest are not; no rule of its own merges either; and nothing is a chart

  Scenario: Robot Framework's pods meet the restricted level with a read-only root and no service account token, and its browser runs without a privilege
    Given the robot application built as Flux applies it, its namespace as the lab-sto2 overlay builds it and the suites of both pieces
    When the namespace's labels, the exceptions list, the pods of the CronJob and the Deployment and the suites' text are read
    Then the namespace enforces restricted, keeps its objects when it leaves the tree and has no entry in the exceptions list; each pod passes the restricted level, as non-root with the runtime's seccomp profile, mounts no service account token, takes no Service environment variables and sets no host namespace, host alias or DNS setting of its own; the runner runs as user, group and file system group 1001, changing the group of its claim only when its root does not have it, and the server as 65534; each container has a read-only root filesystem, no capability and no privilege escalation; the runner mounts the suites, the scripts and the optional Ascender suite read-only, the claim at /reports and a memory emptyDir with a size limit at /tmp, and nothing at /dev/shm; the server mounts the claim read-only and nothing writable; the runner requests CPU within 500m and memory in a multiple of 64Mi within 1Gi, is limited in memory to twice its request within 2Gi and not in CPU; the server requests 1m and 8Mi and is limited to 32Mi; and no suite or resource file sets chromiumSandbox

  Scenario: Robot Framework's namespace denies traffic by default, its runner reaches the resolver and its targets and nothing else, and its report server takes only the host
    Given the render of the tree, with its real charts
    When the namespace's NetworkPolicy, Robot Framework's three CiliumNetworkPolicies, hello's and Ascender's web policy and the flows of Robot Framework's pods are read and evaluated
    Then the namespace holds the default deny and is not listed; the runner takes nothing and reaches the cluster's resolver on 53, hello's pods on 8080 and Ascender's web pods on 8052, and not the world, the API server, the nodes, the secret store, another port of the resolver, of hello or of Ascender, Ascender's task pods, the proxies, Locust's master, n8n, another application or the report server; the report server takes the host on 8080 and no pod or entity else, and sends nothing; hello takes the runner on 8080 beside the proxies, Locust's workers and the kubelet, and Ascender's web pods take it on 8052 beside the proxies, the kubelet and the task pods, and neither takes the report server or another pod of robot

  Scenario: Robot Framework's targets are Services of the cluster read from the render, and each admits only the runner on its port
    Given the render of the tree, with its real charts, the cluster's DNS domain from its machine configuration, and the robot application built in a tree without Ascender
